# Regression test: terminate on a job wrapping an external command (started via
# `cmd`) killed the real OS process fine, but the job's own bookkeeping stuck around in
# jobs forever afterward. Not specific to termination either -- a background job
# that simply finished on its own, never terminated and never even `fg`'d, leaked in
# exactly the same way when run non-interactively (as any .crush script is).
#
# Root cause: `ThreadStore::spawn` (src/lang/threads.rs) records a `ThreadData` entry
# per spawned thread that embeds a `CommandHandle`, and so a `JobHandle` clone -- kept
# alive in that registry until something explicitly calls `reap()`/`join_one()` for it,
# never automatically just because the underlying OS thread already returned. The
# interactive REPL calls `threads().reap(...)` after every line specifically to clean
# this up, but running a script through `execute::file`/`execute::string` never did, so
# that lingering clone kept `remove_finished_jobs`' `strong_count()` check
# (global_state.rs) from ever seeing the job as dead, no matter how long it waited or how
# many other jobs got created afterward.
#
# Fixed by calling `threads().reap(...)` from `create_job_handle_with_parent` itself --
# the same place `remove_finished_jobs` already runs on every new job's creation -- so
# reaping now happens lazily and uniformly regardless of whether crush is being driven
# interactively or by a script.
$job_id := $(cmd sleep 8171 &)
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)
terminate $job_id
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=500)

# Matched by a distinctive sleep duration rather than by id: ids get recycled (see
# tests/job_orphaning_on_parent_death.crush), so the very next job created here --
# this jobs pipeline itself -- could plausibly be handed $job_id's own freed id,
# and filtering on `id == $job_id` would then wrongly match jobs' own still-running
# row instead of correctly finding nothing.
$remaining_rows := $(jobs | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 8171"} | materialize)
$remaining_count := $($remaining_rows | count)
assert ($remaining_count == 0) "a terminated external-command job should eventually be reaped from jobs, not linger forever"
echo "done"
